window.TIBER_SEARCH = [{"title": "Framework overview", "category": "Concepts", "url": "methodology-overview.html", "text": "Methodology — overview How we deliver a TIBER-EU engagement. This is our operating model, grounded in the normative framework ) but written as practice. Three levels of the process Always keep these distinct — mixing them causes governance failures: 1. Regulatory governance — who initiates, controls and formally recognises the TLPT . Owner of the engagement is the financial entity , not us. 2. Threat Intelligence — who researches threats and forms threat scenarios . 3. Red Team Operations — who turns scenarios into attack plans, executes and evidences . A TIBER test is not a large penetration test . It is a controlled simulation of attacks on critical/important functions that assesses compromise and the defender's ability to detect and respond. Our delivery model: single provider, separated teams The client has agreed one provider supplies both TIP and RT, with internal separation of the two teams. To make that separation auditable we maintain a TI-to-RT Handover Register recording each artifact, who prepared it, who reviewed it, and its approval status. This is a traceability tool, not a substitute for the formal TIBER approval procedure. Guiding principles - Intelligence-led, not tool-led. Scenarios come from real, relevant threat actors — never invented exploit chains promised up front. - Scope and risk belong to the client. The RTTM advises on technical feasibility; the CT/CTL own the SSD and the authority validates it. - Safety first. Testing runs on production; every risky action has approvals, stop conditions and deconfliction. See safety-and-risk.md . - Evidence by default. Timestamped, attributable, chain-of-custody evidence for every flag and significant TTP — captured continuously, not reconstructed afterwards. - Success is measured against objectives, not Domain Admin. Detection and response outcomes are part of the result. - Everything maps to MITRE ATT&CK for a shared language across TTIR, RTTP and RTTR. Phase model See engagement-lifecycle.md for the narrative and ../phases/ for per-phase activities, inputs, outputs and gates."}, {"title": "Engagement lifecycle", "category": "Concepts", "url": "methodology-engagement-lifecycle.html", "text": "Engagement lifecycle The full arc from regulatory initiation to attestation, from our vantage point. Per-phase detail lives under ../phases/ ; timings in ../reference/framework-timeline.md . 1. Regulatory initiation — Entity is notified to run a TLPT; Authority appoints TM; entity forms CT/CTL and initiation documents. BT is not informed. 2. RFP / procurement — Entity selects TIP and RT . We respond with technical + commercial proposals; independence and qualifications are assessed; contracts signed before testing starts. 3. Scoping & launch — CT/CTL author the SSD , validated by the authority. Launch meeting aligns scope, comms, RoE basics, escalation and schedule. 4. Threat intelligence — TIP gathers target intelligence and threat intelligence , builds a scenario longlist, runs scenario selection , and finalises the TTIR . TTIR is approved by TM. 5. Red team planning — RTTM turns the TTIR into the RTTP : attack paths, TTP mapping, infrastructure, schedule, risk controls, RoE, comms, evidence, contingencies. Active attacks start only after CT + TM approve the RTTP. 6. Active testing — reconnaissance → initial access → discovery/lateral movement → privilege escalation → objective validation against flags. Continuous safety control, approvals for risky actions, weekly status, evidence capture, deviation and leg-up documentation. 7. Reporting, replay & purple team — RTTR , BTTR by BT , joint Replay , then the mandatory Purple Teaming exercise . 8. Remediation & closure — Entity produces TSR and Remediation Plan , 360-degree feedback from the parties, then — once the TIBER authority approves the TSR and RP — the authority signs/issues the attestation , formally closing the test and enabling mutual recognition. Remediation and regulatory follow-up may continue. Key handoffs - TIP → RTTM: TTIR + selected scenarios become the RTTP's foundation. TIP may continue to supply intelligence during active testing. Track via the TI-to-RT Handover Register. - RT → BT: controlled disclosure at Replay; open collaboration at Purple Teaming. - Providers → Entity → Authority: RTTR/BTTR feed TSR/RP, which feed attestation."}, {"title": "Threat intelligence method (TIP)", "category": "Concepts", "url": "methodology-threat-intelligence-method.html", "text": "Threat Intelligence method How the TI stream runs the threat intelligence phase as the Threat Intelligence Provider and produces a defensible TTIR document set : the TTIR + scenario matrix + evidence register . Primary basis : ECB TIBER-EU Targeted Threat Intelligence Report Guidance §2–§3 + Annex 1 ; the binding DORA TLPT RTS Annex III ; and the practical Norges Bank TIBER-NO TTIR Guidance v1.2 . The deliverable's required content is defined in the TTIR template . Recognised CTI tradecraft is used in support of — never instead of — these requirements, and is marked . The output is not a raw OSINT dump . It is contextualised reasoning about which attacks are realistic for this entity: target intelligence and threat intelligence alternate and supplement each other , and must never be analysed in silos. --- A. The TIP engagement — operational sub-phases The TI phase runs as a mini intelligence cycle , instantiated as six working steps inside the ~4–6 week window: A1. Direction — kickoff, questionnaire & intelligence requirements - Inputs: the validated SSD and the launch alignment. The CT and TM have already agreed scope before the TIP starts drafting. - Business-overview session + questionnaire. The entity provides a strategic understanding of itself at the start of the phase . Run this as a structured intake — see the TI scoping questionnaire . Much of it is obtainable from open source, but having the entity supply it lets the TIP spend its effort on analysis and digital-footprint work , not on basic facts. Treat anything commercially sensitive pragmatically. - Set intelligence requirements . Turn the SSD CIFs/flags into explicit questions the collection must answer . - Agree OPSEC & deconfliction rules : the TI phase must not alert the blue team . A2. Collection — target + threat intelligence Run the two streams in parallel . Target: digital footprint on a per-CIF basis . Threat: GTL + actor research. Passive by default ; any active reconnaissance is tightly gated . A3. Processing & analysis — contextualise, then prioritise actors - Contextualise the CIFs — enrich the SSD CIF list with what GTL, business overview, footprint and intel input reveal about why this entity is a target. - Contextualise the flags — translate each SSD flag into how a real actor would reach it . - Threat-actor longlist → shortlist — categorise actors, then name specific actors; score and rank by capability × intent ; document the scoring method and why rejected actors were dropped . A4. Scenario development — longlist → shortlist Build a longlist of high-level scenarios covering"}, {"title": "Red team method (RT)", "category": "Concepts", "url": "methodology-red-team-method.html", "text": "Red Team method How the red team turns an approved TTIR into executed, evidenced, safe testing. 1. From TTIR to RTTP For each selected scenario, design concrete attack paths to the agreed flags, mapped to MITRE ATT&CK. Assemble the RTTP : objectives, scenarios, attack paths, TTP mapping, infrastructure/tooling/comms, execution schedule, risk management, RoE , evidence approach and contingencies/leg-ups. No active attack begins until CT + TM approve the RTTP. 2. Operational cycle 1. Operator proposes an action / attack path. 2. RTTM checks it against RTTP and risk. 3. Actions needing separate authorisation → approval via CT/TM. 4. Operator executes the approved action and captures evidence . 5. RTTM updates attack timeline, flag status and risk register. 6. Weekly meeting: results, blockers, deviations, next steps. 3. Illustrative execution arc Period RT work RTTM control --- --- --- Wk 1–2 Recon, infra prep, validation Safety checks, scenario readiness Wk 3–4 Initial access simulations Risk approvals, monitoring, evidence Wk 5–7 Internal discovery, lateral movement, priv-esc Progress vs flags, escalation Wk 8–10 Access to CIF-supporting systems, objective validation Production impact, objective evidence Wk 11–12 Alternative paths, consolidation, cleanup readiness Coverage review, cleanup Illustrative only — scenarios may run in parallel, repeat, or change after approval. 4. What the RTTM controls continuously - Safety and production impact. - Flags achieved / not achieved. - Successful vs unsuccessful TTPs. - Approved deviations and all leg-ups. - Detection & response observations . - Evidence preservation and confidentiality. - Resources, schedule, inter-scenario dependencies. 5. Success criteria Success is measured against agreed CIF objectives/flags and the defender's detection/response outcomes — not reaching Domain Admin. A blocked path validated via an approved, documented leg-up still tests the subsequent defences; an undocumented shortcut to a flag does not. 6. Reporting Produce the RTTR within 4 weeks of the end of active testing : executed scenarios, attack paths, TTPs, flags, deviations, leg-ups, evidence, vulnerabilities, root causes and recommendations. Support Replay, the mandatory Purple Teaming exercise and the entity's remediation planning."}, {"title": "Safety & risk", "category": "Concepts", "url": "methodology-safety-and-risk.html", "text": "Safety & risk management Testing runs on production . Safety is non-negotiable and the financial entity holds overall responsibility. This file defines the controls we apply as TIP/RT. Non-negotiables - No scope changes by us. The RTTM advises feasibility; CT/CTL own the SSD, authority validates. - No risky action without approval. Anything outside pre-approved RoE needs CT/TM sign-off. - Stop conditions are absolute. When a stop condition triggers, halt and escalate immediately. - Deconfliction always available. CT/TM can confirm in real time whether activity is the test. Stop conditions - Risk of real service disruption to a CIF or its customers. - Unexpected impact on systems/data outside agreed scope. - Discovery of a pre-existing real compromise or live incident. - Safety-of-life, legal, or data-protection concern. - Loss of control over tooling, access or evidence. - Instruction to stop from CT, CTL, TM or authority. On trigger: cease activity → preserve state/evidence → notify CT/TM via the agreed channel → await instruction. Record in the risk register and timeline. Deconfliction procedure Maintain an agreed out-of-band channel and named contacts. When BT/SOC escalates suspicious activity, CT queries the RTTM who confirms whether it is the test — so a real incident is never masked, and the test is never mistaken for a breach. Leg-ups A controlled CT-provided assist to validate a later scenario stage when blocked. Rules: - Must be approved by CT/TM and documented . - Must not fabricate reaching a flag — its purpose is to test the next defence. - Recorded in the RTTP contingencies, risk register, evidence register and RTTR. Risk register Keep a living register: risk → likelihood/impact → mitigation → owner → status. Review at every weekly meeting. Significant residual risks are escalated to CT/TM before proceeding. Evidence & confidentiality - Capture timestamped, attributable evidence continuously . - Maintain chain of custody; store securely; restrict to need-to-know. - Never store real client data in this repository — only fictional examples. - Clean up implants/infrastructure/accounts per an agreed cleanup plan; confirm with CT."}, {"title": "Red team guides — index", "category": "Red Team guides", "url": "red-team.html", "text": "Red Team guides library Reusable red team manuals and guides that extend the engagement-specific red team method with general tradecraft, planning and operating practice. These are written to be lifted into engagements and refined over time. Everything here is original content , authored for this repository and aligned to the TIBER-EU / DORA operating model . Where a classic reference says it better, we cite it rather than copy it. Contents Guide What it covers --- --- Concepts What red teaming is; red team vs pen test vs vuln scan; threat emulation; team colours &amp; TIBER mapping Goal planning Engagement goals, objectives, success criteria; mapping to CIFs/flags; measures of success vs performance Tradecraft &amp; OPSEC C2 &amp; infrastructure, redirectors, OPSEC, evasion principles, deconfliction, attribution hygiene — with the TIBER safety overlay Threat-profile emulation Turning a TTIR actor into an emulation plan Operator log What to record per action, and why; schema that feeds the evidence register and RTTR Engagement checklist Phase-by-phase go/no-go checklist for operators and the RTTM How this fits the engagement - Plan a scenario → goal planning + threat-profile emulation → feeds the RTTP . - Operate safely → tradecraft &amp; OPSEC + oplog within safety &amp; risk and the RoE . - Evidence &amp; report → oplog → evidence register → RTTR / finding cards . Further reading These informed our topic coverage; consult them directly for their own treatment: - RedTeam.Guide — <https://redteam.guide . - Vest, J. &amp; Tubberville, J., Red Team Development and Operations: A Practical Guide — the book behind that site. - MITRE ATT&CK — <https://attack.mitre.org/ · MITRE Engenuity CTID adversary emulation plans. - CREST red team / threat-intelligence guidance; CBEST / TIBER-EU official texts ). We summarise widely-known concepts in our own words and link out for detailed treatments; we do not copy text from copyrighted sources into this repository."}, {"title": "Concepts (red vs pen vs vuln)", "category": "Red Team guides", "url": "red-team-concepts.html", "text": "Red team concepts Original overview of red teaming and where it sits among security-testing types, aligned to the TIBER-EU / DORA operating model. For detailed outside treatments see the further reading . What red teaming is Red teaming is objective-driven adversary emulation : a trusted team reproduces the tactics, techniques and procedures of a realistic threat to test whether an organisation can prevent, detect and respond to it — end to end, across people, process and technology . Success is not \"did we get Domain Admin\"; it is what the defence saw and did against agreed objectives. In TIBER-EU this is formalised as flags tied to Critical or Important Functions . Engagement lifecycle In a TIBER-EU engagement this generic arc is governed by CT/TM and maps onto the formal phases — see the TIBER-EU lifecycle and phase guides . Red teaming vs penetration testing vs vulnerability assessment Each manages a different kind of risk; they are complementary, not substitutes. Aspect Vulnerability assessment Penetration test Red team engagement --- --- --- --- Primary goal Enumerate known weaknesses Prove exploitability of specific flaws Measure detection &amp; response against a realistic adversary Scope Broad, shallow Defined targets/systems Objective-led, whole-org attack surface Focus Technology Technology People + process + technology Knowledge Usually credentialed/known Grey/known Intel-led, low prior knowledge Defender awareness Aware Often aware Unaware Threat model Known CVEs/misconfig Reachable attack surface Adversary emulation Typical duration Hours–days ~1–2 weeks Weeks–months Measure of value Coverage / exposure reduced Exploit impact demonstrated Resilience: prevent / detect / respond outcomes In TIBER-EU the comparison matters for scoping conversations: a TLPT is a red team engagement , not a pen test. Avoid promising specific compromises up front — scenarios are intelligence-derived later in the TTIR . Team colours and the TIBER mapping - Red — the offensive team . - Blue — the defenders ; in a TIBER test they are not told it is happening. - White → Control Team — governs, approves risk and deconflicts; in TIBER the CT/CTL with the authority-side Test Manager . - Purple — structured red+blue collaboration; mandatory in the TIBER closure phase. Threat-led vs scenario-based - Threat-led — scenarios derive from intelligence about actors that realistically target the entity . - Scenario X — at most one forward-looking, non-threat-led scenario . See goal planning for turning objectives into flags, and threat-profile emulation for turning an actor into"}, {"title": "Goal planning", "category": "Red Team guides", "url": "red-team-goal-planning.html", "text": "Goal planning Original guide to setting red-team objectives and turning them into testable flags, aligned to TIBER-EU. Planning quality determines whether a test produces learning or just activity. Goals → objectives → flags - Goal — the business question the entity wants answered . - Objective — the concrete thing the red team must demonstrate . - Flag — the agreed, evidenced proof an objective was met, tied to a specific CIF ; refined in the TTIR and RTTP ). - Success criteria — exactly what proves the flag . Good objectives are CIF-anchored and safe-by-design Property Why it matters --- --- Tied to a CIF Keeps the test on systemically important function, not a trophy host Impact-named States the aspect proven — confidentiality / integrity / availability / authenticity Evidenced Defines the artefact that proves it Bounded Says how to prove impact without causing it Detection-relevant The path to it exercises defences worth measuring Measures: outcome vs performance - Measures of Success — were objectives/flags reached? . - Measures of Performance — how did the defence perform along the way? time-to-detect, time-to-respond, which controls fired, what was missed. MoP is where most learning lives and feeds Replay / Purple Teaming and the BTTR . Success is measured against agreed objectives and defender detection/response — never \"Domain Admin\". A flag reached via an approved, documented leg-up still validates the later defences; an undocumented shortcut does not. See safety &amp; risk . Planning checklist - Each CIF in scope has at least one candidate objective. - Objectives cover availability, integrity and confidentiality across the scenario set. - Every flag has a written success criterion and a \"prove-without-causing-impact\" method. - Learning goals from the entity are captured and reflected in scenario selection. - Leg-up triggers and deadlines are pre-agreed . Next: threat-profile emulation · tradecraft &amp; OPSEC · the RTTP template . --- Concept credit: goal/objective-based red-team planning as popularised by Joe Vest &amp; James Tubberville — see RedTeam.Guide and their book ). Text here is original and TIBER-aligned."}, {"title": "Tradecraft & OPSEC", "category": "Red Team guides", "url": "red-team-tradecraft-and-opsec.html", "text": "Tradecraft & OPSEC Original guide to red-team operational tradecraft and operations security, with the TIBER safety overlay that governs a regulated TLPT. Tradecraft here means operating like a real adversary while staying safe, controlled and deconflicted. Operating picture Infrastructure - Separation &amp; redirection — operators never touch targets directly; traffic flows through redirectors to C2 so infrastructure is resilient and attributable only to the engagement. - Attribution hygiene — categorised domains, aged infrastructure, TLS, realistic user-agents; avoid patterns that trivially burn the operation on day one. - Segmented tooling — distinct infra per scenario where noise/sophistication differs, so one burnt channel doesn't collapse the whole test. - Channel diversity — primary + fallback C2 so a single detection doesn't end access prematurely. OPSEC principles - Least noise for the goal — match tradecraft to the emulated actor; don't be louder than the profile unless a scenario intends it. - Know your indicators — understand what each tool/action emits ; prefer living-off-the-land where the profile fits. - Evidence as you go — timestamped capture of each action ); OPSEC includes being able to prove what you did and did not do. - Data minimisation — collect only what proves the flag; use test tokens/canaries, never real customer data; handle any sensitive artefact per the agreed data rules. The TIBER safety overlay - Blue team stays unaware — nothing should reveal the test; coordination is with the Control Team , not the defenders. - Production-safe — no action outside the RoE / RTTP; destructive impact is demonstrated, not caused . - Deconfliction — a standing channel to the CT to confirm \"was that us?\" and to pause on real incidents or unexpected risk. - Leg-ups — when a path is blocked and continuation isn't safely/meaningfully possible, request a documented leg-up so later defences are still tested. - Stop conditions — defined up front; the CTL can suspend the test; limited purple teaming may be used to continue safely. See safety &amp; risk . Evasion — principle, not recipe Emulate the detection-evasion sophistication of the profiled actor to test whether controls detect that class of behaviour — the aim is to measure the defence , not to \"win\". Tie every evasive choice back to the threat profile and the learning goal, and record it for the Replay/Purple discussion. Related: threat-profile emulation · operator log · red team method . --- Concept credit: red-team tradecraft &amp; OPSEC practice as popularised by Joe Vest &amp; James Tubbe"}, {"title": "Threat-profile emulation", "category": "Red Team guides", "url": "red-team-threat-profile-emulation.html", "text": "Threat-profile emulation Original guide to turning a TTIR threat actor into an emulation plan the red team can execute — the bridge between intelligence and operations. It consumes the actor profiles from the TTIR and feeds the RTTP . From actor to emulation plan What a threat profile captures Field Content --- --- Actor / archetype Named actor or class being emulated Motivation &amp; intent Why they target this entity / CIF Capability Sophistication, resources, custom vs commodity tooling Signature TTPs Concrete ATT&CK techniques the actor is known/assessed to use Initial-access preference Phish, exploit public-facing, supply chain, valid accounts… Post-exploitation style Direct-to-objective vs broad presence; stealth vs speed Tooling to emulate Representative C2 / tooling of comparable behaviour \"Do\" list Behaviours in-profile to reproduce \"Don't\" list Out-of-profile behaviours to avoid Attribution tells Characteristic indicators worth reproducing to test detections Backward- vs forward-looking Emulate evidence-based behaviour but, as the TTIR allows, add plausible forward-looking TTPs — keeping the two clearly distinguished, and keeping flexibility for any Scenario X . Fidelity vs feasibility Aim for the highest fidelity that is safe and feasible in the window. Where exact tooling is impractical or unsafe, emulate the behaviour and indicators that exercise the same defences — the goal is to measure detection/response of that TTP class, not to run the actor's actual malware. Emulation stays inside the RoE and safety &amp; risk rules. Each emulated TTP maps to a flag/CIF and is recorded in the oplog for the Replay/Purple discussion. --- Concept credit: adversary-emulation and threat-profile practice as popularised by Joe Vest &amp; James Tubberville — see RedTeam.Guide and their book ). Text here is original; consult the originals for their treatment."}, {"title": "Operator log (oplog)", "category": "Red Team guides", "url": "red-team-operator-log.html", "text": "Operator log Original guide + schema for the operator log — the contemporaneous, timestamped record of every action the red team takes. The oplog is the backbone of evidence: it feeds the evidence register , the RTTR storyline, and the Replay/Purple reconstruction. Why it matters - Reconstruction — Replay and the BTTR compare red actions to blue detections per step ; without a clean oplog there is nothing to align against. - Deconfliction — answers \"was that us?\" instantly during the test. - Safety &amp; accountability — proves what was and was not done, and that actions stayed within the RoE . - Attribution of detections — lets the blue team learn which exact action they did/didn't see. Log every action as it happens Schema Field Example / note --- --- Oplog ID OL-0007 Timestamp 2026-09-03T14:22:07Z — single source of truth timezone Operator handle/initials Scenario SC-A Phase in / through / out Source host redirector / C2 / workstation Target host / service / identity TTP T1566 Phishing · T1021 Remote Services Action / command what was run Result success / fail / partial + brief outcome Detection expectation what this should trip Evidence ref EV-012 Flag impact progresses/achieves FLAG-1, or none Risk / notes leg-up used, deconfliction raised, cleanup needed Discipline - Contemporaneous — write it as you act, not after; memory is not evidence. - One UTC clock — so red and blue timelines align exactly. - Sanitise, don't omit — record enough to reconstruct; keep real secrets/customer data out . - Track cleanup — flag every artefact that must be removed; confirm removal at closure. Keep the live oplog as a spreadsheet/structured file in the engagement workspace, not in this repository. The evidence register template defines the companion evidence columns. --- Concept credit: operator-log / activity-log practice as popularised by Joe Vest &amp; James Tubberville — see RedTeam.Guide and their book ). Text and schema here are original."}, {"title": "Engagement checklist", "category": "Red Team guides", "url": "red-team-engagement-checklist.html", "text": "Engagement checklist Original, condensed go/no-go checklist for operators and the RTTM across a TIBER-EU engagement. It complements the formal phase guides — use it as a quick operational gate, not a replacement for the gate/exit criteria there. Before active testing starts - Contracts/NDA/insurance signed; team separation documented. - Project charter + SSD agreed; CIFs &amp; flags validated. - TTIR approved; CT + TM attested ; ≥3 scenarios . - RTTP + RoE approved by CT + TM . - Leg-up triggers/deadlines, stop conditions and deconfliction channel agreed. - Infrastructure stood up &amp; validated; oplog + evidence store ready. - Blue team confirmed unaware ; need-to-know list current. During active testing - Action is within RTTP &amp; RoE and within risk appetite . - Evidence captured; oplog updated ; flag status &amp; risk register current. - Deconfliction used on any ambiguity or real-incident signal. - Weekly sync: progress vs flags, blockers, deviations, leg-ups, next steps. - Noise/sophistication matched to the emulated threat profile . Closure - All cleanup/artefact removal confirmed. - RTTR delivered ≤4 weeks ; findings written with root cause + priority. - Support BTTR , replay and the mandatory purple teaming . - Input to TSR &amp; remediation plan; 360° feedback given. This is an operator aide-mémoire. The binding sequence, timings and gates live in the phase guides and the framework timeline . --- Concept credit: red-team checklist practice as popularised by Joe Vest &amp; James Tubberville — see RedTeam.Guide and their book ). This checklist is original and TIBER-aligned."}, {"title": "Phase 0 · Pre-engagement / RFP", "category": "Guides · Phases", "url": "phases-00-pre-engagement-rfp.html", "text": "Phase 0 — Pre-engagement / RFP / Procurement Timing: before or M0–M6 · Owner: Authority/TM + CT Where our work actually begins as a provider — winning and contracting the engagement. Activities - Regulatory notification; authority appoints TM; entity forms CT/CTL + initiation docs. - Entity issues RFP/RFI for TIP and RT . - We respond with technical + commercial proposals. - Due diligence on qualifications and independence ; vendor selection; contracting. - BT is not informed. Our RFP response package Document Contents --- --- Technical proposal Methodology, execution model, governance Team composition RTTM, operators, specialists, backup resources Experience & references Relevant red-team engagements Methodology statement Threat-led adversary simulation, MITRE ATT&CK, safety Risk management approach Production safety, escalation, stop conditions Delivery plan Phases, milestones, dependencies Commercial proposal Price, resources, scope-change terms Gate / exit criteria - Procurement completed, contracts signed, providers onboarded — before testing starts. Inputs → Outputs - In: RFP/RFI, preliminary scope, objectives. - Out: signed contracts, agreed delivery plan. Watch-outs - Do not promise specific compromises or fixed exploit chains — scenarios are TI-derived later. - Independence/separation must hold even in the single-provider model. - Honestly represent capability . Reusables - Proposal kit → ../../skills/ - Pricing sheet reference → "}, {"title": "Phase 1 · Scoping & launch", "category": "Guides · Phases", "url": "phases-01-scoping-launch.html", "text": "Phase 1 — Scoping & Launch Timing: start of Preparation · Owner: CT/CTL ; we consult Activities - CT/CTL author the Scope Specification Document : CIFs, supporting systems/services/ processes, third-party dependencies, flags , boundaries and exclusions. - Entity leadership approves SSD; authority validates it. - Launch meeting with CT, TM, TIP and RT: scope, plan, comms channels, RoE basics, responsible persons, schedule and escalation procedure. SSD example shape CIF Supporting assets Flag --- --- --- Payment processing Payment gateway, IAM, processing backend Controlled proof of ability to influence the payment process Customer banking Web/API, authentication, backend Proof of unauthorised access to a defined test resource Treasury operations Trading infrastructure, privileged access Proof of reaching an agreed privileged position Gate / exit criteria - SSD approved by entity and validated by authority. - Launch meeting held; emergency-stop and deconfliction understood by all. Inputs → Outputs - In: signed contracts, preliminary scope. - Out: validated SSD, launch alignment, comms/escalation plan. Our role RTTM may advise on technical feasibility of flags/scope but does not own the SSD . Reusables - ../../templates/project-charter-template.md - ../../templates/ssd-template.md"}, {"title": "Phase 2 · Threat intelligence", "category": "Guides · Phases", "url": "phases-02-threat-intelligence.html", "text": "Phase 2 — Threat Intelligence Timing: ~4–6 weeks · Owner: TIP See the full method in ../../methodology/threat-intelligence-method.md . Activities - Direction: business-overview session + questionnaire at the start; set intelligence requirements from the SSD; agree OPSEC/deconfliction . - Collection: target intelligence and threat intelligence . Passive by default ; active recon only by the RTT, at TIP request, after CT+TM approval. - Processing & analysis: contextualise CIFs & flags; actor longlist → shortlist via capability × intent scoring; determine modus operandi mapped to MITRE ATT&CK. - Scenario development: longlist → shortlist , targeting every CIF; profile actors . - Scenario selection meeting: CT, TM, TIP, RT — RTTM assesses feasibility, time, TTP match, flag reachability without unacceptable impact, leg-ups and dependencies; CTL selects the shortlist. - TTIR finalisation , TTIR meeting, flag re-validation ; CT + TM agree/attest before the test proceeds. - Handover to RT ; after the test, share the TTIR with the entity's CTI team. See the full operational method in ../../methodology/threat-intelligence-method.md . Gate / exit criteria - TTIR approved by TM. - ≥3 end-to-end scenarios selected, covering Availability, Integrity, Confidentiality. - Each scenario mapped to a CIF and agreed flag, with evidence references. Inputs → Outputs - In: validated SSD, launch alignment. - Out: approved TTIR , scenario shortlist, actor profiles, evidence register, handover to RT. Reusables - ../../templates/ti-scoping-questionnaire.md - ../../templates/ttir-template.md - ../../templates/threat-actor-scenario-matrix.md - ../../templates/evidence-register.md - ../../templates/ti-to-rt-handover-register.md"}, {"title": "Phase 3 · Red team planning", "category": "Guides · Phases", "url": "phases-03-red-team-planning.html", "text": "Phase 3 — Red Team Planning Timing: ~2–3 weeks · Owner: RT / RTTM See the full method in ../../methodology/red-team-method.md . Activities - Turn approved TTIR scenarios into concrete attack paths to agreed flags. - Build the Red Team Test Plan and Rules of Engagement . - Define infrastructure, tooling, comms, execution schedule, risk controls, evidence approach, contingencies and leg-up procedures. - RTTP meeting: present plan and risks to CT and TM. RTTP contents Objectives · threat scenarios + rationale · attack paths · TTP mapping · infrastructure · execution schedule · risk management · RoE · communications/escalation · evidence · contingencies/leg-ups. Gate / exit criteria - RTTP and RoE approved by CT and TM. Active attacks begin only after this approval. Inputs → Outputs - In: approved TTIR + selected scenarios + evidence . - Out: approved RTTP, approved RoE, readiness to execute. Reusables - ../../templates/rttp-template.md - ../../templates/rules-of-engagement.md - ../../methodology/safety-and-risk.md"}, {"title": "Phase 4 · Active testing", "category": "Guides · Phases", "url": "phases-04-active-testing.html", "text": "Phase 4 — Active Red Team Testing Timing: minimum 12 weeks · Owner: RT / RTTM Realistic, phased attack execution toward agreed flags — not 12 weeks of scanning. Activities Period RT work RTTM control --- --- --- Wk 1–2 Recon, infra prep, validation Safety checks, readiness Wk 3–4 Initial access simulations Risk approvals, monitoring, evidence Wk 5–7 Discovery, lateral movement, priv-esc Progress vs flags, escalation Wk 8–10 Access CIF-supporting systems, objective validation Production impact, evidence Wk 11–12 Alternative paths, consolidation, cleanup readiness Coverage review, cleanup Operational cycle Propose → RTTM checks vs RTTP/risk → CT/TM approval if required → execute + capture evidence → update timeline/flags/risk register → weekly review. Continuous controls Safety/production impact · flags achieved vs not · successful/unsuccessful TTPs · approved deviations · all leg-ups · detection/response observations · evidence preservation + confidentiality · resources/schedule/dependencies. Gate / exit criteria - Agreed scenarios executed , evidence consolidated, cleanup ready. - Success assessed vs CIF objectives/flags and defender detection/response — not Domain Admin. Inputs → Outputs - In: approved RTTP + RoE. - Out: operational timeline, evidence set, observations feeding RTTR and Replay. Reusables - ../../templates/evidence-register.md - ../../methodology/safety-and-risk.md"}, {"title": "Phase 5 · Reporting, replay & purple", "category": "Guides · Phases", "url": "phases-05-reporting-replay-purple.html", "text": "Phase 5 — Reporting, Replay & Purple Teaming Timing : RTTR ≤ 4 weeks · BTTR ≤ 10 weeks · Replay ≤ 10 weeks · Purple Teaming ≤ 10 weeks — all from the end of active testing. Owner: RT + BT + CT Controlled disclosure to the Blue Team and learning activities. Activities - RTTR : executed scenarios, attack paths, TTPs, flags, deviations, leg-ups, evidence, vulnerabilities, root causes, recommendations. Delivered to the CT within 4 weeks of the end of active testing. - BTTR : telemetry, alerts, incident tickets, response actions vs RT timeline. Within 10 weeks ; completed ahead of the replay to maximise learning. - Replay: RT + BT reconstruct the attack timeline — what the attacker did, what the SOC saw, what was missed, which alerts fired without response, what could have stopped it. ≤10 weeks. - Purple Teaming : open RT+BT collaboration — what other steps the RT could have taken and how the BT can improve detection/response. ≤10 weeks. Basis: ECB TIBER-EU Purple Teaming Guidance, Jan 2025 . The guidance defines two distinct variants — always be explicit about which one is meant: - Limited Purple Teaming — in the testing phase : used only under exceptional circumstances to avoid impact on data, damage to assets, or disruption to CIFs / the entity / its counterparts / the sector; only after the CTL has suspended the test , when continuation is not otherwise possible, and after prior TM approval . LPT counts toward the 12-week minimum active-testing duration. - Purple Teaming exercise — in the closure phase : a standard, mandatory element, no later than 10 weeks after active testing ends. Topics are jointly chosen by key stakeholders and may include: vulnerabilities found during the test · issues that could not be tested during active testing · other steps the RTT could have taken and potential BT responses · alternative scenarios and their consequences · proofs of concept · anticipated remediation measures discussed with the RTT · business-continuity exercises. Gate / exit criteria - RTTR delivered ≤4 wks; BTTR ≤10 wks ; Replay and Purple Teaming completed ≤10 wks. - Detection/response gaps documented with recommendations. Inputs → Outputs - In: operational timeline + evidence from Phase 4; BT telemetry. - Out: RTTR, BTTR, replay & purple outcomes — inputs to TSR and Remediation Plan. Reusables - ../../templates/rttr-template.md - ../../templates/finding-template.md - ../../templates/bttr-template.md"}, {"title": "Phase 6 · Remediation & closure", "category": "Guides · Phases", "url": "phases-06-remediation-closure.html", "text": "Phase 6 — Remediation, Closure & Attestation Timing : TSR ≤ 8 weeks and Remediation Plan ≤ 8 weeks . Owner: Entity/CT ; attestation signed/issued by the TIBER authority . Activities - Entity produces the Test Summary Report and Remediation Plan , delivered to the TM. - 360-degree feedback from the principal parties. - Once the TIBER authority has approved the TSR and Remediation Plan , it signs/issues the attestation confirming the test followed the core TIBER-EU requirements → formal closure and grounds for mutual recognition. Deliverables & timing Deliverable Owner Timing --- --- --- Test Summary Report Entity / CT to TM within 8 weeks Remediation Plan Entity to TM within 8 weeks 360-degree feedback All parties before final TSR/RP approval Attestation TIBER authority after TSR + RP approved Remediation Plan must contain Concrete actions · priorities · responsible owners · expected timelines · risks of non-completion. Our role Provide remediation input from RTTR findings: root-cause-oriented recommendations, prioritisation, and a cyber-resilience improvement roadmap. Each finding carries its priority and root cause on its finding card , so the entity can lift them straight into the Remediation Plan. We do not own the TSR/RP, and in the 2025 framework the attestation is signed/issued by the TIBER authority — not by us. Gate / exit criteria - TSR and RP approved by the authority; attestation signed/issued by the TIBER authority. Remediation execution and regulatory follow-up may continue afterward. Inputs → Outputs - In: RTTR, BTTR, replay/purple outcomes. - Out: TSR, RP, 360° feedback, attestation . Reusables - ../../templates/tsr-template.md - ../../templates/finding-template.md - Attestation content — see ../../reference/deliverables-matrix.md"}, {"title": "Project charter (RTS Annex I)", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-project-charter-template.html", "text": "Project Charter — TEMPLATE Basis: Commission Delegated Regulation 2025/1190 , Annex I — \"Content of the project charter )\" . New under the RTS — this is a binding preparation-phase deliverable that did not exist as a named artifact in the pre-DORA TIBER-EU framework. Owner: Control Team Lead / entity. We are named in it and consulted, but do not own it. Produced in the preparation phase . Copy per engagement; do not edit this blank. --- Annex I compliance spine Item of information Information required --- --- Person responsible for the project plan — i.e. the Control Team Lead Name · contact details Testers ☐ internal ☐ external ☐ both Communication channels and Art. 9 ) email encryption to be used · online data rooms to be used · instant messaging to be used Codename for the TLPT CIFs operated in other Member States 1. list of CIFs operated in another Member State · 2. for each, the Member State in which they are operated CIFs supported by ICT third-party service providers 3. list of such CIFs · 4. for each, identification of the ICT third-party service provider Expected deadlines for completion of: &nbsp;&nbsp; Preparation Phase yyyy-mm-dd &nbsp;&nbsp; Testing Phase yyyy-mm-dd &nbsp;&nbsp; Closure Phase yyyy-mm-dd &nbsp;&nbsp; Remediation Plan yyyy-mm-dd Timing context : preparation ceilings and the active red team testing phase ≥ 12 weeks ). The charter's deadlines must be consistent with the framework timeline . --- Supporting sections - Document control — version, classification, author , TM awareness, distribution, change history. - Governance & stakeholders — CT members, TM / TLPT authority, TIP, RT , and the single-provider separation arrangement ). - Risk management summary — link to the RoE and safety-and-risk . - Links — this charter precedes and frames the SSD ; the SSD's scope must reconcile with the CIFs listed here."}, {"title": "Scope Specification Document (Annex II)", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-ssd-template.html", "text": "Scope Specification Document — TEMPLATE Basis: ECB TIBER-EU Scope Specification Document Guidance, January 2025 , §2 \"Required content\" + Annexes 1–3, cross-checked against the binding DORA TLPT RTS, Annex II 2025/1190, ../assets/source/Delegated-Regulation-EU-2025-1190-DORA-TLPT-RTS.md ). ⚠️ The RTS adds authenticity to the flag security aspects , where the ECB guidance listed only C/I/A. Owner: CT/CTL . Validated by: the authority / TM . We may consult on technical feasibility only. Not just a list of IPs. Copy per engagement; do not edit this blank. --- Part A — ECB §2 compliance spine - A1 — A list of ALL CIFs identified by the financial entity . - A2 — Per CIF, an inclusion/exclusion rationale: where in scope, the reasons for its inclusion ; where not in scope, the reasons for its exclusion . - A3 — The identified ICT system supporting each CIF. - A4 — Per ICT system: whether it is outsourced and, if so, the name of the ICT third-party service provider ; and the jurisdiction in which the system is used. - A5 — A high-level description of preliminary flag , indicating which security aspect — confidentiality, integrity, authenticity or availability — each flag covers . Annexes in the ECB guidance to reuse: Annex 1 executive-summary template · Annex 2 CIF / subfunction / justification template · Annex 3 flag-setting template. --- Part B — Drafting layout 1. Document control Version · classification · author · approvals · authority/TM validation · distribution. 2. Critical or Important Functions List every CIF the entity has identified; for each, state whether it is in scope and the explicit reason for inclusion , or out of scope and the explicit reason for exclusion . 3. CIF → supporting ICT systems → flags CIF Supporting ICT system Outsourced? → ICT third-party provider Jurisdiction of use Preliminary flag + security aspect --- --- --- --- --- Payment gateway, IAM, processing backend Yes → provider name DE, SK Controlled proof of ability to influence the payment process — Integrity Web/API, authentication, backend No SK Proof of unauthorised access to a defined test resource — Confidentiality Trading infrastructure, privileged access Yes → provider name SK, LU Proof of reaching an agreed privileged position / disrupting a process — Availability 4. In-scope environment Production systems/services, external attack surface , people/locations in scope. 5. Exclusions & boundaries Out-of-scope systems, prohibited actions, third-party systems not to be actively tested. 6. Risk controls & constraints Testing windows, change freezes, data-handling rules, legal"}, {"title": "TI scoping questionnaire", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-ti-scoping-questionnaire.html", "text": "TI Scoping Questionnaire & Business-Overview Intake — TEMPLATE Basis: ECB TTIR Guidance, Jan 2025, Annex 1 \"Business overview input\" + §3.2 . This is the structured intake the entity provides to the TIP at the start of the TI phase , plus the TIP's own direction-setting . It feeds the TTIR business-overview and digital-footprint sections and the threat-actor assessment. Owner: completed by the entity ; structured and analysed by the TIP . Commercially sensitive items: handle pragmatically; keep real data out of this repository . --- Part 1 — Business overview input - The entity & its CIFs — explanation of the entity and its critical or important functions and their significance for the broader financial sector. - Entity's own threat assessment — including examples of recent adverse cyber events. - Systemic implications — potential impact of a compromise of the entity's confidentiality, integrity and availability. - Business model & org setup — shareholder ownership, company structure, Board & executive management, products & services, key financial figures. - Geographic presence — countries in which the entity operates. - Interdependencies — financial and operational; countries from which the entity receives significant supply-chain support . - Client types of potential interest to foreign intelligence . - Niche markets the entity is active in. - R&D / IP — high-level insight into any niche research knowledge or intellectual property. - M&A — high-level insight into possible future mergers/acquisitions that may raise actor interest. - Geopolitical exposure — issues or investments that may affect the threat landscape. - Third-party involvement in CIFs — details. - Technical anchors — the entity's domains and IP addresses . Rationale : most of this is available from open source, but entity-provided input lets the TIP concentrate effort on analysis and digital-footprint discovery rather than basic fact-finding. Part 2 — CIF / systems detail CIF Sub-functions Supporting key systems/services ICT third parties Jurisdiction Associated flag + aspect --- --- --- --- --- --- This must reconcile with the SSD and the project charter . The TIP does not re-scope — it contextualises what the CT/TM already agreed. Part 3 — Session logistics & OPSEC - Business-overview session: attendees , date, format, classification. - Deconfliction & secrecy: confirm the Blue Team is not informed ; agree the codename and need-to-know list; agree encrypted comms / data room . - Sensitivity handling: which items are commercially sensitive and how they'll be shared. - Points of contact: "}, {"title": "TTIR (Annex III)", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-ttir-template.html", "text": "Targeted Threat Intelligence Report — TEMPLATE Basis: ECB TTIR Guidance, January 2025 , §2 \"Required content\", mirroring the binding DORA TLPT RTS, Annex III 2025/1190, ../assets/source/Delegated-Regulation-EU-2025-1190-DORA-TLPT-RTS.md ) — the non-threat-led Scenario X is the one referred to in Art. 10 . Owner: TIP. Assessed by CT and TM. Classification: fill in. Copy per engagement; do not edit this blank. Written during the TI phase, finalised after scenario selection ; at the end, re-validate the SSD flags with the entity. --- Part A — ECB §2 compliance spine Use this checklist to prove completeness; the report sections in Part B carry the detail. - A1 — Overall scope of the intelligence research , including at least: CIFs in scope · their geographical location · official EU language in use · relevant ICT third-party service providers · the period over which research was gathered. - A2 — Overall assessment of concrete actionable intelligence , e.g.: employee usernames/passwords · look-alike domains · technical reconnaissance · employee social-media info · information for sale on the dark web · other relevant internet/public-network info · where relevant, physical targeting information . - A3 — Threat intelligence analysis of the general threat landscape and the entity's particular situation, including at least: geopolitical environment · economic environment · technological and other sector-relevant trends. - A4 — Threat profiles of the malicious actors that may target the entity: systems most likely to be targeted · motivation, intent and rationale · likely modus operandi. - A5 — Threat scenarios: at least three end-to-end scenarios for the profiles with the highest threat severity scores , each describing the full end-to-end attack path, with at least one covering availability , one integrity , and one confidentiality . - A6 — Scenario X — optionally, one of the three selected scenarios may be a non-threat-led, forward-looking Scenario X . Intelligence gathering may use OSINT and HUMINT with strong ethical conduct, and must not alert the Blue Team . Active reconnaissance by the RTT in the TI phase is allowed only at the TIP's request and after CT + TM approval. Share the TTIR with the entity's CTI team after the test to help them reduce targetable information. --- Part B — Report structure Structure informed by the ECB guidance and the Norges Bank example. Each section cites which Part A element it satisfies. 1. Document Control & Confidentiality — version, classification, author , reviewers, approvals, distribution, change history. 2. Executive Su"}, {"title": "Threat-actor & scenario matrix", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-threat-actor-scenario-matrix.html", "text": "Threat Actor & Scenario Matrix — TEMPLATE Owner: TIP. Companion to the TTIR. In a real engagement keep this as .xlsx ; this .md defines the required columns and an illustrative row. Threat actor prioritisation Actor ID Actor / group Type Capability Intent Relevance Score Confidence Rationale Selected? --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- TA-01 Cybercrime Scoring methodology: define the scale and weighting; record in the evidence register. Threat scenarios Field Example — fictional bank --- --- Scenario ID SC-01 Threat Actor Financially motivated intrusion group Target CIF Payment Processing Motivation Financial gain Entry Vector Credential phishing Key TTPs Initial Access, Credential Access, Lateral Movement Objective Controlled demonstration of unauthorized payment-system access Flag FLAG-PAY-01 Impact Dimension Integrity Intelligence Confidence Medium Supporting Evidence TI-SRC-014, TI-SRC-021 Illustrative only — a real scenario needs confirmed sources, actor rationale and a full attack flow. Scenario table Scenario ID Threat actor Target CIF Entry vector Impact dim. Flag Confidence Selected? Reason if rejected --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- SC-01 Completeness check: selected scenarios collectively cover A + I + C ; ≥3 total; ≤1 Scenario-X."}, {"title": "Evidence register", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-evidence-register.html", "text": "Evidence Register — TEMPLATE Owner: TIP + RT . In a real engagement keep as .xlsx . This defines required columns. Never store real client data in the repository. TI source references Source ID Type Description URL / location Date collected Confidence Used in --- --- --- --- --- --- --- TI-SRC-001 Scoring methodology Document the actor-scoring scale, weighting and confidence definitions here so prioritisation is repeatable. IOC / TTP table ID Type Value / technique MITRE ATT&CK ID Context Source ref --- --- --- --- --- --- IOC-001 Execution evidence Evidence ID Timestamp Scenario Attack step TTP Description Artifact location Operator Flag ref --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- EV-001 Chain of custody For each key artifact: who captured it, when, where stored, access controls, retention/destruction plan."}, {"title": "TI-to-RT handover register", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-ti-to-rt-handover-register.html", "text": "TI-to-RT Handover Register — TEMPLATE Owner: TIP + RTTM. Makes the single-provider team separation auditable. A traceability tool, not a substitute for the formal TIBER approval procedure. Copy per engagement. Handover log Artifact Prepared by Reviewed by Approval / Status Version Date --- --- --- --- --- --- Threat Actor Profiles TI Lead CT / TM Reviewed Scenario Longlist TI Team CT / TM Selection pending Final TTIR TI Lead CT / TM Approval required Evidence Register TI Team RTTM Transferred RTTP RTTM CT / TM Approval required Open questions / clarifications Question Raised by To Status Resolution --- --- --- --- --- --- 1 Decisions log Decision Rationale Decided by Date --- --- --- --- --- 1 Separation attestation Confirm TI and RT responsibilities remained separated during handover; note any shared personnel and the controls applied."}, {"title": "Red Team Test Plan (Annex IV)", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-rttp-template.html", "text": "Red Team Test Plan — TEMPLATE Basis: ECB TIBER-EU Red Team Test Plan Guidance, January 2025 , §2 \"Required content\" + §3 considerations, mirroring the binding DORA TLPT RTS, Annex IV 2025/1190, ../assets/source/Delegated-Regulation-EU-2025-1190-DORA-TLPT-RTS.md ). Owner: RT / RTTM. Approved by CT + TM before any active attack. Copy per engagement; do not edit this blank. Built from the approved SSD + TTIR + selected scenarios. --- Part A — ECB §2 compliance spine Prove completeness here; the drafting sections in Part B carry the detail. - A1 — Communication channels and procedures to be used. - A2 — TTPs allowed and not-allowed in the attack, including ethical boundaries for social engineering and how the privacy of involved parties is safeguarded . - A3 — Risk management measures to be followed by the RTT. - A4 — A description for each scenario , including: the simulated threat actor · their intent, motivation and goals · the target function and supporting ICT system · the targeted confidentiality, integrity, availability and authenticity aspects · the flags . - A5 — A detailed description of each expected attack path , including pre-requisites and possible leg-ups to be provided by the CT, with deadlines for their provision and potential usage. - A6 — Scheduling of red teaming activities , with time planning per scenario, at minimum split into the three tester phases: in — entering the entity's ICT systems · through — moving through them and executing actions on objectives · out — extracting from them. - A7 — Particularities of the entity's infrastructure to be considered during testing. - A8 — Any additional information or resources the testers need to execute the scenarios. Red team composition : disclose the full RTT composition, each member's experience, roles and contact details; appoint a red team manager + deputy ; document that competencies match the scope. Any change to RTT composition must be communicated to the TM and CTL and captured in a revised RTTP. --- Part B — Drafting layout 1. Document control Version · classification · author · reviewers · CT approval · TM approval · distribution · change history. 2. Objectives CIFs · attack objectives · flags · success criteria . 3. Threat scenarios Selected scenarios from the TTIR with rationale. 4. Attack paths Per scenario: technical path to the flag, prerequisites, decision points, alternatives. 5. TTP mapping MITRE ATT&CK mapping and adversary tradecraft per path. 6. Infrastructure & tooling Red team infrastructure, C2, tooling, communications, data handling. 7. Execution schedule Phases, sequ"}, {"title": "Rules of Engagement", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-rules-of-engagement.html", "text": "Rules of Engagement — TEMPLATE Owner: RT / RTTM, agreed with CT/TM. Attached to / referenced by the RTTP. Copy per engagement. 1. Parties & contacts RTTM · operators · CTL · CT · TM. Named contacts + out-of-band deconfliction channel . 2. Testing windows Permitted days/hours, blackout/change-freeze periods, time zone. 3. Permitted actions Explicitly allowed techniques, targets, and scope boundaries. 4. Prohibited actions Explicitly disallowed techniques . 5. Approval requirements Which actions need separate CT/TM approval before execution; how approval is requested/recorded. 6. Stop conditions & emergency stop List of stop conditions; emergency-stop procedure: cease → preserve → notify CT/TM → await. .) 7. Deconfliction procedure How CT confirms whether activity is the test; response window; escalation. 8. Data handling & confidentiality How evidence/data is captured, stored, transmitted, retained and destroyed; need-to-know. 9. Leg-ups When/how leg-ups may be requested; approval + documentation requirements; constraints. 10. Communications & status cadence Weekly status format, attendees, channels; incident/urgent escalation. 11. Cleanup Removal of implants/infrastructure/accounts; confirmation to CT. 12. Sign-off RTTM · CT · TM · date/version."}, {"title": "Red Team Test Report (Annex V)", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-rttr-template.html", "text": "Red Team Test Report — TEMPLATE Basis: ECB TIBER-EU Red Team Test Report Guidance, January 2025 , §2 \"Required content\" + §3 considerations, mirroring the binding DORA TLPT RTS, Annex V 2025/1190, ../assets/source/Delegated-Regulation-EU-2025-1190-DORA-TLPT-RTS.md ). Owner: RT / RTTM. Due to the CT within 4 weeks of the end of active testing . Feeds TSR and Remediation Plan. Copy per engagement; do not edit this blank. --- Part A — ECB §2 compliance spine - A1 — Information on the performed attack , including: the targeted CIFs and the ICT systems/processes/technologies supporting them · a summary of each scenario · flags reached and not reached · attack paths followed successfully and unsuccessfully · TTPs used successfully and unsuccessfully · deviations from the RTTP · leg-ups granted . - A2 — All actions the testers are aware of that were performed by the BT to reconstruct the attack and mitigate its effects. - A3 — Discovered vulnerabilities and other findings , including: description and criticality · root-cause analysis of successful attacks · recommendations for remediation , each with a remediation-priority indication. §3 considerations: write a management summary in layman's terms for senior management/Board; a per-scenario storyline ; then findings , provisional root cause analysis , recommendations , artefacts , and a confidentiality handling note. --- Part B — Drafting layout 1. Document control Version · classification · author · reviewers · distribution · change history. 2. Executive summary Outcome vs objectives/flags · key risks · headline detection/response observations · priority recommendations. 3. Scope & methodology CIFs, flags, scenarios, approach; reference to SSD, TTIR, RTTP. 4. Executed scenarios Per scenario: narrative, attack path taken, result , timeline. 5. TTPs used Mapped to MITRE ATT&CK; successful vs unsuccessful; what was detected. 6. Flags Status of each flag with evidence references. 7. Deviations & leg-ups All approved deviations and leg-ups: what, why, when, who approved. 8. Evidence Key evidence with timestamps and references to the evidence register. 9. Vulnerabilities & root causes Findings, severity, root-cause analysis . Draft one card per finding with the finding template and roll them up into the finding register here. 10. Detection & response observations What the defence saw/missed . 11. Recommendations Root-cause-oriented, prioritised; input to the entity's Remediation Plan; resilience roadmap. 12. Appendices Detailed evidence, IOC/TTP tables, tool/infrastructure notes, cleanup confirmation."}, {"title": "Finding card + register", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-finding-template.html", "text": "Finding — TEMPLATE One card per finding . Findings are the atomic unit that rolls up into the RTTR and feeds the entity's Remediation Plan . Owner: RT / RTTM. Copy this block per finding; do not edit this blank. A TIBER finding is not only a CVE/misconfiguration — it can be a control, process or people gap . Rate by business impact , not by whether Domain Admin was reached. Keep real client data out of the repository. --- Finding register ID Title Criticality CIF / scenario Flag ref Remediation priority Status --- --- --- --- --- --- --- F-001 Critical/High/Medium/Low/Info FLAG-/SC- P1/P2/P3 Open/Remediated/Accepted --- F-00X — <short title Field Value --- --- Finding ID F-00X Title <concise, impact-first Criticality Critical / High / Medium / Low / Informational Rating basis business impact × likelihood ; CVSS v3.1/v4.0 vector Category vulnerability / misconfiguration / control gap / detection gap / process / people / third-party CIF affected <critical or important function Scenario SC-0X Security aspect Confidentiality / Integrity / Availability / Authenticity Flag linkage FLAG-0X — reached / not reached / partial Affected systems hosts / apps / services / cloud resources Discovered <UTC date Status Open / In remediation / Remediated / Risk accepted Summary One or two sentences in plain language: what the weakness is and why it matters to the business. Attack path / reproduction Ordered steps the RTT took, split by in / through / out phase where relevant. Each step maps to a MITRE ATT&CK technique ID and cites execution evidence from the register. 1. Txxxx step … — evidence EV-00X 2. Txxxx.yyy step … — evidence EV-00Y Evidence Reference the evidence register IDs . Do not paste raw client data here — point to the artifact store. - EV-00X — <what it proves Impact - Business impact: effect on the CIF / service / data / regulatory exposure. - Technical impact: access, data, or control the attacker gained. - Realism / effort: skill, time and pre-requisites a real actor would need . Detection & response - Detected? Yes / No / Partial — at which step, by which control. - Response: what the BT did, time-to-detect / time-to-respond, what was missed. Root-cause analysis The underlying cause, not the symptom . Note contributing systemic/process factors. Recommendation & remediation priority - Priority: P1 / P2 / P3 . - Recommendation: root-cause-oriented, actionable, assigned-ownership-ready; short-term fix vs strategic resilience improvement where they differ. References CWE / CVE / MITRE ATT&CK / vendor advisory / standard as applicable. --- Criticality scale L"}, {"title": "Blue Team Test Report (Annex VI)", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-bttr-template.html", "text": "Blue Team Test Report — TEMPLATE Basis: Commission Delegated Regulation 2025/1190 , Annex VI — \"Content of the blue team test report )\" . Owner: the entity's Blue Team — client-produced . We do not author it; this blank is kept so the RT/CT know what the BTTR must contain, can align the RTTR's attack-step structure to it, and can quality-check it before replay/purple. Produced in the closure phase , after the RTTR; completed ahead of the replay to maximise learning. --- Annex VI compliance spine - 1 — Per attack step described by the testers in the RTTR: - list of detected attack actions ; - log entries corresponding to these detections. - 2 — Assessment of the testers' findings and recommendations. - 3 — Evidence of the attack . - 4 — BT root-cause analysis of the testers' successful attacks. - 5 — Lessons learned and identified potential for improvement. - 6 — List of topics to be addressed in purple teaming. The BTTR is a per-attack-step mirror of the RTTR : it maps what the defence saw against each RTT action. Keep the RTTR attack-step/TTP numbering stable so the BT can reference it 1:1. Item 6 directly seeds the closure-phase purple teaming . --- Drafting layout 1. Document control Version · classification · author · reviewers · distribution · change history. 2. Detection & response per attack step RTTR step Attack action Detected? Detection source / control Log entry reference Response taken Time-to-detect / respond --- --- --- --- --- --- --- Yes / No / Partial 3. Assessment of RT findings & recommendations BT view on each RTTR finding: agree / partially agree / dispute, with reasoning and any additional context. 4. Attack evidence collected by the BT Telemetry, alerts, tickets, PCAPs, EDR traces the BT captured — referenced, not pasted raw. 5. Root-cause analysis of successful attacks BT's view of why detections/responses failed . 6. Lessons learned & improvement potential Prioritised people / process / technology improvements. 7. Topics for purple teaming Specific items to replay or explore collaboratively in the closure-phase purple exercise."}, {"title": "Test Summary Report (Annex VII)", "category": "Templates", "url": "templates-tsr-template.html", "text": "Test Summary Report — TEMPLATE Basis: Commission Delegated Regulation 2025/1190 , Annex VII — \"Details of the report summarizing the relevant findings of the TLPT of Regulation 2022/2554)\" . Owner: the financial entity / CT — client-produced . We provide substantial input . Delivered to the TM within 8 weeks of the end of active testing; the TM's approval of the TSR + Remediation Plan is the precondition for the attestation . Copy per engagement; do not edit this blank. --- Annex VII compliance spine - Parties involved — CT/CTL, TM/TLPT authority, TIP, RT, BT; third parties where relevant. - The project plan — per the project charter . - Validated scope — incl. the rationale for inclusion/exclusion of CIFs and the ICT systems/processes/technologies supporting them ). - Selected scenarios and any significant deviation from the TTIR . - Executed attack paths and the TTPs used. - Captured and non-captured flags. - Deviations from the RTTP , if any. - Blue team detections , if any. - Purple teaming in the testing phase — where conducted, and the related conditions . - Leg-ups used , if any. - Risk management measures taken. - Identified vulnerabilities and other findings , including their criticality . - Root-cause analysis of successful attacks. - High-level remediation plan — linking each vulnerability/finding to its root cause and remediation priority . - Lessons derived from feedback received . The TSR is the consolidated, authority-facing summary of the whole TLPT. It reuses, rather than repeats, the detailed artifacts: pull findings from the finding cards / RTTR, detections from the BTTR , and scope rationale from the SSD. Keep it summary-level; the evidence lives in the underlying reports. --- Drafting layout 1. Document control & confidentiality — version, classification, authors, approvals, distribution. 2. Parties & governance . 3. Project plan & timeline — key dates, active-testing duration . 4. Validated scope & rationale . 5. Scenarios & TTIR deviations . 6. Execution summary — attack paths, TTPs, flags captured/not, RTTP deviations, leg-ups. 7. Defensive outcomes — BT detections; purple teaming in the testing phase + conditions. 8. Risk management measures . 9. Findings & root causes — vulnerabilities/findings with criticality; root-cause analysis. 10. High-level remediation plan — findings ↔ root causes ↔ priority . 11. Lessons learned . Companion deliverable: the Remediation Plan — concrete actions, owners, timelines and non-completion risks — also to the TM within 8 weeks. The attestation ) follows the TM's approval of TSR + RP."}, {"title": "DORA & TIBER-EU — maps & tables", "category": "Reference", "url": "reference-dora-tiber-maps.html", "text": "DORA & TIBER-EU — maps & tables Visual quick-reference for the regulation and the framework: mindmaps and tables that summarise how DORA 2022/2554), its TLPT RTS 2025/1190) and TIBER-EU fit together. Diagrams are Mermaid . DORA at a glance TLPT in DORA — the key articles Article Subject --- --- Art. 24 General principles for digital operational resilience testing Art. 25 Testing of ICT tools and systems Art. 26 Advanced testing based on TLPT — ≥ every 3 years; covers CIFs on live production; attestation &amp; mutual recognition Art. 27 Requirements for TLPT testers TIBER-EU is the ECB framework used to satisfy the DORA TLPT obligation. See the full texts: DORA · TLPT RTS . The TLPT RTS — binding deliverable Annexes Annex Deliverable Our template --- --- --- I Project charter project-charter II Scope Specification Document ssd III Targeted Threat Intelligence Report ttir IV Red Team Test Plan rttp V Red Team Test Report rttr VI Blue Team Test Report bttr VII Test Summary Report tsr VIII Attestation — TIBER-EU engagement lifecycle See the phase guides and framework timeline . Who's who Full ownership detail: roles &amp; RACI . Mandatory timings Stage Ceiling / minimum --- --- Preparation ≤ 6 months Threat intelligence ~4–6 weeks Red team planning ~2–3 weeks Active red team testing ≥ 12 weeks RTTR ≤ 4 weeks BTTR / Replay / Purple teaming ≤ 10 weeks TSR / Remediation Plan ≤ 8 weeks Diagrams are editable text — update the Markdown and re-run python3 site/build site.py ."}, {"title": "Framework timeline (2025)", "category": "Reference", "url": "reference-framework-timeline.html", "text": "Framework timeline & timings Reconciled against primary sources : ECB TIBER-EU Framework 2025 , ECB TTIR Guidance Jan 2025 , and Commission Delegated Regulation 2025/1190 . The 2025 framework was updated to align with the DORA TLPT RTS, so these timings are now framework-level and regulation-aligned. Mandatory / defined timings Item Requirement Source --- --- --- Preparation phase no longer than 6 months from the start of the test Framework 2025 Threat Intelligence phase indicative 4–6 weeks Framework 2025 RT test plan creation indicative 2–3 weeks Framework 2025 Active red team testing minimum 12 weeks Framework 2025; Reg. 2025/1190 Art. 11 Red Team Test Report delivered to CT within 4 weeks of end of active testing Framework 2025 Blue Team Test Report within 10 weeks of end of active testing Framework 2025 Replay exercise within 10 weeks of end of active testing Framework 2025 Purple Teaming exercise within 10 weeks of end of active testing Framework 2025 Test Summary Report delivered to TM within 8 weeks Framework 2025 Remediation Plan delivered to TM within 8 weeks Framework 2025 DORA RTS applicability adopted 13 Feb 2025 · OJ 18 Jun 2025 · applies from 8 Jul 2025 Reg. 2025/1190 Verify exact RTS article references against the OJ text before quoting to a client — the regulation PDF could not be auto-downloaded ). Scenarios - At least three end-to-end threat scenarios, for the threat profiles with the highest threat severity scores , each describing the full end-to-end attack path, covering at least: service availability , data integrity , and information confidentiality . - At most one of the three selected scenarios may be a non-threat-led Scenario X . Phase sequence Phases commonly overlap . Phase → folder map Phase Folder --- --- 0 Pre-engagement / RFP ../phases/00-pre-engagement-rfp/ 1 Scoping & launch ../phases/01-scoping-launch/ 2 Threat intelligence ../phases/02-threat-intelligence/ 3 Red team planning ../phases/03-red-team-planning/ 4 Active testing ../phases/04-active-testing/ 5 Reporting / replay / purple ../phases/05-reporting-replay-purple/ 6 Remediation & closure ../phases/06-remediation-closure/"}, {"title": "Roles & RACI", "category": "Reference", "url": "reference-roles-and-raci.html", "text": "Roles & RACI Who owns what in a TIBER-EU 2025 / DORA TLPT engagement. Critical distinction: TM ≠ RTTM . We never own scope or risk sign-off. Naming : Control Team , Control Team Lead , Test Manager . The legacy 2018 names were White Team / WTL / TTM. See acronyms.md . Role owners Role Who it is Primary responsibility --- --- --- TIBER/TLPT Authority Regulator / empowered body Oversight, validation, signs/issues the attestation TCT — TIBER Cyber Team Authority-side team Coordinates/oversees the TIBER process in the jurisdiction TM — Test Manager Member of the TCT Ensures process conformance; manages the test on the authority side CTL — Control Team Lead Client representative End-to-end coordination from the entity side CT — Control Team Limited client group Scope, approvals, risk, communications TIP — Threat Intelligence Provider External/our TI stream Targeted threat intelligence & threat scenarios RTTM — Red Team Test Manager Our red team lead Technical plan, team, execution, reporting RTT — Red Team Testers Our operators Attack simulation and evidence collection BT — Blue Team SOC, IR, defenders Detection & response, without prior warning of the test The financial entity holds overall responsibility for test safety. The RTTM cannot unilaterally change scope, authorise a risky action, or substitute for the authority's TM. In the 2025 framework the attestation is signed/issued by the TIBER authority after it approves the TSR and Remediation Plan; it provides the grounds for mutual recognition . Control Team responsibilities Source: ../assets/source/ecb-control-team-guidance-2025.pdf . The CT owns the end-to-end conduct of the test and manages the phases safely, in close collaboration with — and direct contact with — the TM throughout. Its main responsibilities: - Execute the test in compliance with TIBER-EU; keep the entity's management body informed of progress and associated risks. - Ensure risk-management controls are in place and effective, keeping business impact within the entity's risk appetite . - Involve all relevant stakeholders so the CIFs are captured in the SSD for a realistic simulation. - Facilitate procurement of the TIP and external RTT per the Service Provider Procurement guidance; where internal RTT are used, evaluate suitability, ensure requirements are met, and obtain TM approval . - Liaise closely with TIP/RTT and the TM across the lifecycle; define communication channels and information protocols . - Keep execution within the agreed scope and rule on any requested deviation. - Be responsible for the timely delivery of all deliverab"}, {"title": "Deliverables matrix", "category": "Reference", "url": "reference-deliverables-matrix.html", "text": "Deliverables matrix Every artifact across the engagement, its owner and when it is produced. Template links point to the blank, reusable version in ../templates/ . Deliverable / Artifact Owner When Template --- --- --- --- RFP / tender package CT / Procurement Preparation — Technical & commercial proposal TIP / RT Procurement skills/ Contracts / NDA / SOW / MSA Entity + providers Before testing — Initiation documents CTL Preparation — Project charter CTL / Entity Preparation project-charter-template.md Scope Specification Document CTL / Entity Scoping ssd-template.md TI scoping questionnaire / business-overview intake Entity → TIP TI phase ti-scoping-questionnaire.md Targeted Threat Intelligence Report TIP TI phase ttir-template.md Threat actor & scenario matrix TIP TI phase threat-actor-scenario-matrix.md Evidence register TIP / RT TI + testing evidence-register.md Scenario shortlist TIP + CT/TM/RT input TI phase Red Team Test Plan RT / RTTM RT planning rttp-template.md Rules of Engagement RT / RTTM RT planning rules-of-engagement.md TI-to-RT handover register TIP + RTTM TI→RT handoff ti-to-rt-handover-register.md Operational timeline / evidence RT Active testing evidence-register.md Red Team Test Report RT / RTTM Closure rttr-template.md Finding cards + register RT / RTTM Closure finding-template.md Blue Team Test Report BT Closure bttr-template.md Replay & purple team outcomes RT + BT + CT Closure — Test Summary Report Entity / CT Closure tsr-template.md Remediation Plan Entity Closure — 360° feedback All parties Closure — Attestation TIBER authority Completion — Our owned deliverables : TTIR, scenario matrix, evidence register, RTTP, RoE, TI-to-RT handover, RTTR, finding cards, and input to TSR/RP. Build and refine these templates here so each engagement starts from a mature baseline. Attestation — content The attestation is issued by the TIBER/TLPT authority after it approves the TSR and Remediation Plan DORA). It must contain, at least: on the performed TLPT — start/end dates, CIFs in scope, CIFs in scope not actually tested , other financial entities involved, participating ICT third-party providers, whether internal testers were used and whether Art. 5 2nd subpara was used, and the active red team testing duration in calendar days ; where several TLPT authorities were involved, the others and in which capacity; the list of documents the authority examined for the attestation. It is the basis for mutual recognition between competent authorities."}, {"title": "Official sources", "category": "Reference", "url": "reference-official-sources.html", "text": "Official sources & references Normative documents. This repo paraphrases; the live documents are the source of truth. Documents downloaded locally live in ../assets/source/ — see SOURCES.md . Verify current versions before relying on specifics in a client deliverable. Primary framework & regulation Document Publisher / date Local file Use --- --- --- --- --- 1 TIBER-EU Framework ECB, 2025 ecb-tiber-eu-framework-2025.pdf ✅ Full lifecycle, roles, deliverables, mandatory timings ; purple-teaming mandatory; CT/TM terms 2 TIBER-EU Guidance for Service Provider Procurement ECB, Jan 2025 ecb-service-procurement-2025.pdf ✅ How TIP/RT providers are evaluated and selected 3 Commission Delegated Regulation 2025/1190 EU — adopted 13 Feb 2025, OJ 18 Jun 2025, applies 8 Jul 2025 Delegated-Regulation-EU-2025-1190-DORA-TLPT-RTS.md ✅ Binding TLPT requirements. 17 articles + 8 Annexes = the mandated content of the project charter, SSD, TTIR, RTTP, RTTR, BTTR, findings-summary report & attestation. Active testing ≥ 12 wks ) 4 DORA — Regulation 2022/2554 EU, 14 Dec 2022 DORA-Regulation-EU-2022-2554.md ✅ Parent regulation; TLPT obligation and definitions . Offline Markdown: 64 articles, TOC, footnotes — converted from the OJ XHTML via the EU Publications Office CELLAR endpoint — TIBER-EU Framework 2018 & Procurement 2018 ECB archived-2018/… ⚠️ Superseded — do not use. Historical comparison only ECB per-deliverable guidance These are the normative \"required content\" + \"drafting format\" guidance notes the 2025 framework points to. Each is the basis for the matching template/phase in this repo. Document Local file Basis for --- --- --- --- 4a Scope Specification Document Guidance — §2 required content; Annex 1 exec-summary, Annex 2 CIF/subfunction justification, Annex 3 flag-setting templates ecb-ssd-guidance-2025.pdf ✅ templates/ssd-template.md , phase 01 4b Red Team Test Plan Guidance — §2 required content; §3 org/composition/comms/risk/leg-ups/attack-phase/scenarios; §4 format ecb-rttp-guidance-2025.pdf ✅ templates/rttp-template.md , phase 03 4c Red Team Test Report Guidance — §2 required content; §3 mgmt summary/storyline/findings/root-cause/recommendations/artefacts; §4 format ecb-rttr-guidance-2025.pdf ✅ templates/rttr-template.md , phase 05 4d Purple Teaming Guidance — §2 requirements; §3 limited PT vs PT , types of PT ecb-purple-teaming-guidance-2025.pdf ✅ phase 05 4e Control Team Guidance — §2 responsibilities; §3 composition, CTL/external CTL, skills, governance, escalations, NDA ecb-control-team-guidance-2025.pdf ✅ reference/roles-and-raci.md , phase 01 Threat Intell"}, {"title": "Glossary", "category": "Definitions", "url": "reference-glossary.html", "text": "Glossary — definitions & standards Working definitions for terms that carry specific meaning in TIBER-EU / DORA TLPT. For acronyms see acronyms.md . Threat-Led Penetration Testing A controlled, intelligence-led simulation of real adversary behaviour against a financial entity's critical or important functions . Unlike ordinary penetration testing, scenarios are derived from threat intelligence about real actors and the test assesses not only whether compromise is possible but the organisation's ability to prevent, detect and respond . TIBER-EU The ECB's framework defining how an intelligence-led red-team test is governed, scoped, executed and closed. It is the recognised method EU authorities use to deliver DORA TLPT. Critical or Important Function A business function whose disruption would materially impair the financial entity's services, regulatory compliance or soundness. CIFs anchor scope — scenarios and flags map back to them. Flag A pre-agreed, concrete objective proving an attacker reached a defined level of impact on a CIF . Flags are defined in the SSD and revalidated during TI. Scope Specification Document Client-owned document defining CIFs, supporting systems/services/processes, third-party dependencies, flags, boundaries and exclusions. Not just a list of IPs. Targeted Threat Intelligence Report TIP deliverable: generic + targeted threat landscape, digital footprint, prioritised threat actors with profiles, and selected end-to-end threat scenarios mapped to MITRE ATT&CK. ECB 2025 requires ≥3 end-to-end scenarios covering availability, integrity and confidentiality. Threat scenario A high-level narrative: threat actor → objective → entry vector → key TTPs → target CIF → flag → impact dimension. It is attack logic , not yet an approved technical attack plan . Scenario X A non-threat-led , forward-looking scenario investigating future or otherwise relevant attack vectors , customised to the entity. Per the TIBER-EU 2025 framework, a maximum of one of the three selected scenarios may be a Scenario X . If none is specified during the TI phase, at most one scenario may be transformed into a Scenario X during active testing, with the agreement of the TM, CT and RTT. Generic Threat Landscape A national/sector threat-landscape report that some TIBER-XX jurisdictions produce, covering geopolitical and criminal threats unique to the country. Where a GTL exists, the TIP uses it as foundational input to the TTIR; where none exists, the TIP develops the generic landscape itself. Threat severity score The TIP's prioritisation output ranking threat profile"}, {"title": "Acronyms", "category": "Definitions", "url": "reference-acronyms.html", "text": "Acronyms — TIBER-EU / DORA TLPT Authoritative acronym reference. Terminology source: ECB TIBER-EU Framework and DORA Regulation 2022/2554 + Commission Delegated Regulation 2025/1190. Reconciled against primary PDFs in ../assets/source/ . ⚠️ Terminology changed in Feb 2025. The current TIBER-EU 2025 framework uses Control Team , Control Team Lead and Test Manager . The legacy 2018 framework used White Team , White Team Lead and Team Test Manager . They map 1:1 . Use CT/CTL/TM unless you are deliberately reading an old document. Do not confuse: RTS vs RTT ; RTTP vs RTTR ; TM / TTM vs RTTM . 1. Core framework & roles Acronym Full name Meaning --- --- --- TLPT Threat-Led Penetration Testing Intelligence-led penetration testing TIBER-EU Threat Intelligence-Based Ethical Red Teaming ECB framework/method for intelligence-led red teaming DORA Digital Operational Resilience Act EU regulation on digital operational resilience of the financial sector RT Red Team Team emulating the attacker RTT Red Team Testers Operators executing the simulation RTTM Red Team Test Manager Lead of the red team TI Threat Intelligence Intelligence on cyber threats TIP Threat Intelligence Provider Supplier of targeted threat intelligence CTI Cyber Threat Intelligence The entity's internal CTI team CT Control Team Limited client group that knows about and controls the test CTL Control Team Lead Head of the Control Team WT White Team former name of the Control Team WTL White Team Lead former name of the Control Team Lead BT Blue Team Defenders — unaware during active testing TCT TIBER Cyber Team Team coordinating/overseeing the TIBER process TM Test Manager Authority representative from the TCT who oversees the test TTM Team Test Manager former name of the Test Manager GTL Generic Threat Landscape National/sector threat-landscape report feeding the TTIR PT Purple Teaming Mandatory closure-phase RT+BT collaboration exercise CIF Critical or Important Function Critical/important business function in scope 2. Documents & deliverables Acronym Full name Purpose --- --- --- RFP Request for Proposal Commercial + technical proposal request RFI Request for Information Preliminary information request to providers NDA Non-Disclosure Agreement Confidentiality agreement MSA Master Services Agreement Framework services contract SOW Statement of Work Work, deliverables and responsibilities description SSD Scope Specification Document Defines test scope TTIR Targeted Threat Intelligence Report TI report on threats, actors and scenarios RTTP Red Team Test Plan Plan for conducting the red team test RTTR Red"}, {"title": "DORA — Reg. (EU) 2022/2554", "category": "Regulations", "url": "assets-source-DORA-Regulation-EU-2022-2554.html", "text": "Regulation 2022/2554 — Digital Operational Resilience Act Regulation 2022/2554 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 14 December 2022 on digital operational resilience for the financial sector and amending Regulations No 1060/2009, No 648/2012, No 600/2014, No 909/2014 and 2016/1011 . Source: Official Journal of the European Union, L 333/1, 27.12.2022 , via the EU Publications Office CELLAR endpoint. EU legal texts are freely reproducible . This is an offline reference copy — the authoritative version is the OJ on EUR-Lex. --- Contents - CHAPTER I — General provisions - Article 1 — Subject matter - Article 2 — Scope - Article 3 — Definitions - Article 4 — Proportionality principle - CHAPTER II — ICT risk management - Section I - Article 5 — Governance and organisation - Section II - Article 6 — ICT risk management framework - Article 7 — ICT systems, protocols and tools - Article 8 — Identification - Article 9 — Protection and prevention - Article 10 — Detection - Article 11 — Response and recovery - Article 12 — Backup policies and procedures, restoration and recovery procedures and methods - Article 13 — Learning and evolving - Article 14 — Communication - Article 15 — Further harmonisation of ICT risk management tools, methods, processes and policies - Article 16 — Simplified ICT risk management framework - CHAPTER III — ICT-related incident management, classification and reporting - Article 17 — ICT-related incident management process - Article 18 — Classification of ICT-related incidents and cyber threats - Article 19 — Reporting of major ICT-related incidents and voluntary notification of significant cyber threats - Article 20 — Harmonisation of reporting content and templates - Article 21 — Centralisation of reporting of major ICT-related incidents - Article 22 — Supervisory feedback - Article 23 — Operational or security payment-related incidents concerning credit institutions, payment institutions, account information service providers, and electronic money institutions - CHAPTER IV — Digital operational resilience testing - Article 24 — General requirements for the performance of digital operational resilience testing - Article 25 — Testing of ICT tools and systems - Article 26 — Advanced testing of ICT tools, systems and processes based on TLPT - Article 27 — Requirements for testers for the carrying out of TLPT - CHAPTER V — Managing of ICT third-party risk - Section I — Key principles for a sound management of ICT third-party risk - Article 28 — General principles - Article 29 — Preliminary assessment of ICT concentration risk at "}, {"title": "DORA TLPT RTS — Reg. (EU) 2025/1190", "category": "Regulations", "url": "assets-source-Delegated-Regulation-EU-2025-1190-DORA-TLPT-RTS.html", "text": "Commission Delegated Regulation 2025/1190 — DORA TLPT RTS Commission Delegated Regulation 2025/1190 of 13 February 2025 supplementing Regulation 2022/2554 with regard to regulatory technical standards specifying the criteria for identifying financial entities required to perform threat-led penetration testing , the use of internal testers, the requirements on test methodology, results/closure/remediation, and supervisory cooperation and mutual recognition. Source: OJ L series, 2025/1190, 18.6.2025 , via the EU Publications Office CELLAR endpoint. This is the binding technical standard under DORA Art. 26 . EU legal texts are freely reproducible ; authoritative version is the OJ on EUR-Lex. --- Contents - Article 1 — Definitions - Article 2 — Identification of financial entities required to perform TLPT - Article 3 — TCT and TLPT Test Managers - Article 4 — Organisational arrangements for financial entities - Article 5 — Risk management for TLPT - Article 6 — Risk management for pooled or joint TLPTs - Article 7 — Selection of TLPT providers - Article 8 — Specificities for pooled or joint TLPTs - Article 9 — Preparation phase - Article 10 — Testing phase: threat intelligence - Article 11 — Testing phase: red team test - Article 12 — Closure phase - Article 13 — Remediation plan - Article 14 — Attestation - Article 15 — Use of internal testers - Article 16 — Cooperation and mutual recognition - Article 17 — Entry into force - ANNEX I — Content of the project charter ) - ANNEX II — Content of the scope specification document ) - ANNEX III — Content of the targeted threat intelligence report ) - ANNEX IV — Content of the red team test plan ) - ANNEX V — Content of the red team test report ) - ANNEX VI — Content of the blue team test report ) - ANNEX VII — Details of the report summarizing the relevant findings of the TLPT referred to in Article 26 of Regulation 2022/2554 - ANNEX VIII — Details of the attestation of the TLPT referred to in Article 26 of Regulation 2022/2554 Notes --- - Official Journal of the European Union - 2025/1190 THE EUROPEAN COMMISSION, Having regard to the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union, Having regard to Regulation 2022/2554 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 14 December 2022 on digital operational resilience for the financial sector and amending Regulations No 1060/2009, No 648/2012, No 600/2014, No 909/2014 and 2016/1011 ^1 , and in particular Article 26 , fourth subparagraph thereof, Whereas: - This Regulation has been drafted in accordance with the TIBER-EU framework and mirrors the methodology, proce"}, {"title": "Repository overview (README)", "category": "About", "url": "README.html", "text": "TIBER-EU Engagement Knowledge Base Reusable knowledge base, methodology and template library for delivering TIBER-EU / DORA TLPT engagements as a service provider . This is a factory , not a live engagement. It holds the standards, definitions, methodologies, references, report examples and reusable skills we instantiate from on each real engagement. Where to start - New to TIBER-EU? Read reference/ , then methodology/ . - Working a phase? Open phases/ . - Producing a deliverable? Copy from templates/ . See CLAUDE.md for the full repository map and operating rules. Scope of our role We act as TIP + RT provider. We are not the Test Manager , the Control Team , or the attestation authority. We own the technical threat-led testing and its reporting under the client's and authority's governance. Status / maintenance - Normative source of truth is always the current ECB/NBS/DORA documents, not this repo. - Keep reference/ aligned when frameworks are revised; version significant changes. - Fictional examples only in examples/ — never store real client data here."}, {"title": "Source documents index", "category": "About", "url": "assets-source-SOURCES.html", "text": "Source documents Primary material downloaded for this knowledge base. Downloaded 2026-10-25 . These are the source of truth; ../../reference/ paraphrases them and cites back here. Current File Document Publisher / date Status --- --- --- --- ecb-tiber-eu-framework-2025.pdf TIBER-EU Framework ECB, 2025 ✅ current ecb-ttir-guidance-2025.pdf TIBER-EU Targeted Threat Intelligence Report Guidance ECB, January 2025 ✅ current ecb-service-procurement-2025.pdf TIBER-EU Guidance for Service Provider Procurement ECB, January 2025 ✅ current ecb-ssd-guidance-2025.pdf TIBER-EU Scope Specification Document Guidance ECB, January 2025 ✅ normative basis for ssd-template.md ecb-rttp-guidance-2025.pdf TIBER-EU Red Team Test Plan Guidance ECB, January 2025 ✅ normative basis for rttp-template.md ecb-rttr-guidance-2025.pdf TIBER-EU Red Team Test Report Guidance ECB, January 2025 ✅ normative basis for rttr-template.md ecb-purple-teaming-guidance-2025.pdf TIBER-EU Purple Teaming Guidance ECB, January 2025 ✅ normative basis for phase 05 purple teaming ecb-control-team-guidance-2025.pdf TIBER-EU Control Team Guidance ECB, January 2025 ✅ normative basis for CT roles/RACI DORA-Regulation-EU-2022-2554.md DORA — Regulation 2022/2554 full text, offline Markdown EU, 14 Dec 2022 ✅ converted 2026-10-26 from OJ XHTML Delegated-Regulation-EU-2025-1190-DORA-TLPT-RTS.md Delegated Reg. 2025/1190 full text — 17 articles + 8 Annexes = mandated content of project charter / SSD / TTIR / RTTP / RTTR / BTTR / findings report / attestation EU, 13 Feb 2025 ✅ converted 2026-10-26 via CELLAR norges-bank-tiber-no-ttir-guidance-v1.2.pdf TIBER-NO Targeted Threat Intelligence Report Guidance v1.2 Norges Bank, 04 Feb 2026 ✅ practical TTIR example structure norges-bank-tiber-no-implementation.pdf TIBER-NO Implementeringsveiledning Norges Bank, Oct 2021 ✅ national example Archived — DO NOT USE File Why kept --- --- ecb-tiber-eu-framework-2018-OUTDATED.pdf Superseded 2018 edition; its own cover says \"this publication has become outdated\". Uses legacy White Team / TTM terms and pre-DORA timings. For historical comparison only. ecb-service-procurement-2018-OUTDATED.pdf Superseded Aug-2018 procurement guidance. ⚠️ The generic ECB URLs served the 2018 editions. The current versions are the dated 2025 filenames above. Reconciliation on 2026-10-25 corrected several errors that the 2018 PDFs had introduced . How DORA was obtained — EUR-Lex bypass EUR-Lex's web frontend returns HTTP 202 with an empty body to curl / wget /WebFetch and every mirror tried . The EU Publications Office CELLAR content-negotiation endpoint is "}, {"title": "TI methodology (HTML)", "category": "Examples", "url": "../examples/ti-methodology.html", "text": "worked fictional example meridian bank ttir threat intelligence"}, {"title": "Worked TTIR — Meridian Bank", "category": "Examples", "url": "../examples/example-ttir-meridian-bank.html", "text": "worked fictional example meridian bank ttir threat intelligence"}, {"title": "Worked TI scoping questionnaire", "category": "Examples", "url": "../examples/example-ti-scoping-questionnaire-meridian-bank.html", "text": "worked fictional example meridian bank ttir threat intelligence"}];